Fictional EP Tracklist

1. Drawer 14 Refuses the Scale

Story & Context: During a survey session, Elin opens map drawer 14 and finds several sheets filed at different scales under the same label. She turns the contradiction into a musical rule: one nyckelharpa ostinato remains fixed while every supporting instrument interprets its tonal center differently. Dawit first treats it as Dorian, then removes the third and lets a suspended dominant color emerge. Jonas sustains a bass-trombone note a semitone below Selam’s sub and glides upward only after sixteen bars.

The percussion remains deliberately minimal: kick, one dry rim, and a relay click every fourth bar. Halfway through, the nyckelharpa dry signal disappears but the sympathetic-string bus remains, making the harmony feel present without a visible source. The track ends when the physical drawer is pushed closed and the metal rollers become the final rhythmic gesture.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Open cadastral drawer labeled 14 containing overlapping maps printed at visibly different scales, nyckelharpa keybox at frame edge, bass-trombone slide reflected in cabinet metal, pale paper, graphite annotations, cool institutional light. Spoken fragments limited to “same drawer,” “different scale,” “leave the line,” and “close fourteen.”

2. Brass Compass, Wrong North

Story & Context: Dawit finds an old brass drafting compass whose hinge has loosened enough that its legs no longer hold a fixed radius. Jonas notices that the clicking hinge has a pitch close to one of Elin’s sympathetic strings. Selam samples the click and places it just before selected kicks, creating a subtle anticipatory pulse.

Elin performs a narrow three-note figure while Dawit answers with single Rhodes tones rather than chords. Jonas plays low trombone notes that repeatedly approach the electronic root but stop slightly flat, creating the sensation that tonal “north” keeps drifting. The central section adds a muted trumpet line built from the same modal cells but delayed by one bar through tape echo.

The climax is nearly static: four instruments sustain or repeat tiny gestures while the compass click disappears completely. The groove feels more unstable without the sample that had been acting as an invisible guide.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Tarnished brass drafting compass lying open on a dark map table beside a penciled north arrow that points slightly off the page grid, Rhodes keys in soft focus, nyckelharpa bow hair, bass-trombone mouthpiece, realistic archival lighting. Spoken fragments limited to “north moved,” “do not correct it,” and “keep the radius loose.”

3. Sympathetic Strings After Lights Out

Story & Context: At the end of a late rehearsal, Selam switches off the fluorescent fixtures row by row while Elin continues bowing one low nyckelharpa note. Each lighting relay produces a dry click, but after the final row goes dark, the instrument’s sympathetic strings remain audibly ringing in the archive.

The track begins from that ending. Relay clicks establish the first rhythmic grid before the kick appears. Dawit enters with a single Rhodes dyad every eight bars, and Jonas uses breath-heavy bass-trombone swells rather than full notes. Elin gradually changes bow pressure while keeping the left-hand fingering nearly static, allowing overtone balance rather than melody to create movement.

In the final minute, every electronic element is removed. One bowed note stops, but the sympathetic strings continue in the dark room. The recording ends before their resonance fully disappears.

